rounds.answer1:
When a zero-day enables remote code execution, the **kernel’s inter-process communication (IPC) interface**—specifically, **Binder (Android) or XPC (iOS)**—becomes critical for intercepting telephony subsystem communications. These interfaces mediate secure, privileged data exchange between system services (e.g., telephony, SMS, call manager) and apps. Exploiting them allows attackers to:  
- Inject malicious code into system processes;  
- Monitor or manipulate call/SMS data in real time;  
- Bypass user permissions and sandboxing.  

Thus, securing and auditing IPC mechanisms is essential to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive telephony data, even when the kernel is compromised.

